[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID Principles (Deep Dive) in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The SOLID principles are a set of five design guidelines for writing maintainable, scalable, and robust software using Object-Oriented Programming (OOP). Originally introduced by Robert C. Martin (Uncle Bob), these principles help developers manage dependencies, reduce code coupling, and ensure that systems can evolve over time without breaking existing functionality."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have one, and only one, reason to change. This means a class should only have a single job or responsibility within the software system."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Bad Example: User class handles both business logic and persistence
class User {
    constructor(public name: string) {}

    saveToDatabase() {
        // Database logic here
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"// Good Example: Separation of concerns
class User {
    constructor(public name: string) {}
}

class UserRepository {
    save(user: User) {
        // Database logic here
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension, but closed for modification. You should be able to add new functionality without altering existing, tested code."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"interface DiscountStrategy {
    calculate(amount: number): number;
}

class RegularCustomerDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    calculate(amount: number): number {
        return amount * 0.1;
    }
}

class VIPCustomerDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    calculate(amount: number): number {
        return amount * 0.2;
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Derived classes must be substitutable for their base classes without altering the correct behavior of the program. If class B is a subtype of class A, then objects of type A may be replaced with objects of type B without breaking the application."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preconditions cannot be strengthened in a subtype."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Postconditions cannot be weakened in a subtype."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Invariants of the supertype must be preserved."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"A client should never be forced to implement an interface that it doesn't use, or clients shouldn't be forced to depend on methods they do not use. It is better to create many small, specific interfaces than a few large, general-purpose ones."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Bad: Fat interface
interface Worker {
    work(): void;
    eat(): void;
}

// Good: Segregated interfaces
interface Workable {
    work(): void;
}

interface Eatable {
    eat(): void;
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"interface MessageSender {
    send(message: string): void;
}

class EmailSender implements MessageSender {
    send(message: string): void {
        // Send email
    }
}

class NotificationService {
    constructor(private sender: MessageSender) {}

    notify(msg: string) {
        this.sender.send(msg);
    }
}"}]
